<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  KEEP CRUCIBLE.CAM ON THE CURRENT CODE, WITH NOBODY PRESENT.

  The Stop hook ships at the end of a Claude session, which is the right moment
  and the wrong guarantee: it only fires if someone was in a session, it only
  fires once, and if that one attempt is interrupted — a laptop lid, a killed
  process, two runs colliding — the live site sits behind the code until the
  next session happens to end. That is exactly how crucible.cam came to be
  serving a build from before the Durable Object landed while every local check
  said everything was fine.

  So the same script also runs on a timer. It is a RECONCILER rather than a
  deploy command: it asks production what it is running, compares that to the
  source here, and does nothing at all when they agree. The idle cost is one
  HTTPS request per tick.

  A TEMPLATE, NOT THE INSTALLED FILE. `__CRUCIBLE_ROOT__` is substituted with
  wherever this checkout actually lives at install time, because a hard-coded
  home directory is a watchdog that stops working the day the repo moves — and
  it stops working SILENTLY, which is the one thing a watchdog must never do.

  Install (or reinstall after a move):
    scripts/install-watchdog.sh

  It substitutes the path, loads the job, and then proves it is registered
  rather than assuming `launchctl load` meant anything.

  Remove:
    launchctl unload ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.crucible.deploy.plist
    rm ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.crucible.deploy.plist
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.crucible.deploy</string>

  <!--
    `bash -lc` rather than the script directly: the deploy needs `npm`, `npx`
    and `node` on PATH, and launchd hands a job an almost empty environment. A
    login shell picks up whatever nvm/homebrew arrangement this Mac actually
    has, instead of this file guessing at a path that will be wrong after the
    next Node upgrade.
  -->
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/bash</string>
    <string>-lc</string>
    <string>__CRUCIBLE_ROOT__/scripts/deploy.sh</string>
  </array>

  <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
  <string>__CRUCIBLE_ROOT__</string>

  <!--
    Fifteen minutes, and the number is chosen against the WORST case rather than
    the common one.

    The common run is a single HTTPS request and could fire every minute. But a
    run that actually has work to do takes about eight — the browser test suite
    is most of it — and a ten-minute interval leaves almost no margin before the
    next tick lands on top of it. The lock makes an overlap harmless, and an
    interval that routinely produces overlaps is still a bad interval: it turns
    the normal case into contention and makes the logs hard to read.
    Fifteen leaves the slow path comfortable room and still corrects a missed
    Stop hook long before he next picks up his phone.
  -->
  <key>StartInterval</key>
  <integer>900</integer>

  <!--
    AND ONCE AT LOAD. The most likely moment for the live site to be behind is
    right after the machine wakes or logs in, having missed however many
    intervals it was asleep for.
  -->
  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    Explicitly NOT KeepAlive. This is a periodic reconciler that exits, not a
    daemon; KeepAlive would restart it in a tight loop the moment it finished.
  -->
  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>__CRUCIBLE_ROOT__/.crucible-watchdog.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>__CRUCIBLE_ROOT__/.crucible-watchdog.log</string>

  <!--
    Politeness. The deploy runs a browser test suite, and a background job has
    no business competing with whatever he is actually doing.
  -->
  <key>Nice</key>
  <integer>5</integer>
  <key>LowPriorityIO</key>
  <true/>
  <key>ProcessType</key>
  <string>Background</string>
</dict>
</plist>
